# Append-only log files: two branches each appending new lines should merge as the
# union of both sides' lines, never as a text conflict. Safe here specifically because
# every consumer (tools/agent-monitoring/{generate_retro,query,validate}.py, and the
# agent-ops-dashboard ingest design) reads these by explicit ts/seq/run_id fields, never
# by physical line position — see conversation 2026-07-16 for the verification.
#
# CAVEAT (TCK-20260906-WORKING-LOG-MERGE-UNION-DUPLICATION-GAP): merge=union only
# engages when git's own merge machinery actually runs (`git merge`, `git rebase`,
# `git cherry-pick -m`, GitHub's "Create a merge commit" option) — it does NOT protect
# against a GitHub squash-merge, which this repo's PRs use almost exclusively (5/5 of
# the last 5 merged PRs sampled were squash-merged; a squash-merge has exactly one
# parent and applies a plain diff, invoking no merge driver at all). This gap already
# produced one confirmed ~1586-row whole-block duplication in tickets/working_log.csv
# (see investigation.md for that ticket). The actual squash-merge-safe protection is
# tests/integrity/test_no_duplicate_content_blocks.py, which detects (not prevents)
# any new duplicate content block landing via this path.
#
# CAVEAT (TCK-20260911-WORKING-LOG-LINE-ENDING-UNION-DUPLICATION): merge=union only
# unions cleanly when both sides' additions are byte-identical. A line-ending mismatch
# between writers (e.g. tools/agent-monitoring/record_hand_orchestrated_closure.py's
# csv.writer defaulting to CRLF while every other writer emits LF) makes the *same*
# appended rows look like two different additions to the merge, and merge=union then
# keeps both — a real recurring defect, not a hypothetical one (3 of 4 batch PR merges
# in 3 days duplicated a block this way; see that ticket's investigation.md). `eol=lf`
# is added here specifically to close that gap: it normalizes every writer's output to
# LF at commit time, so two additions that differ only in line ending become byte-
# identical before the merge driver ever sees them, and the union collapses to one
# copy instead of two. tests/integrity/test_merge_union_no_cr_bytes.py catches the
# cause (a stray CR byte) directly, ahead of the next merge that would turn it into a
# duplicate block; tests/integrity/test_merge_union_crlf_duplication_repro.py proves
# both the defect and the fix against a real throwaway git repo.
# Interim rule if you land on a duplicate block before a fix like this one merges: keep
# the copy whose bytes match `main` and delete the other — deleting the copy that
# *doesn't* match main just re-triggers the same duplication on the next such merge.
agent-monitoring/data/*/*.jsonl text eol=lf merge=union
tickets/working_log.csv text eol=lf merge=union

# docs/REGISTRY.yaml is a full-file rewrite on every Finalize (not an append), so
# union-merging two independent regenerations could produce invalid YAML -- deliberately
# NOT merge=union. Instead: `make setup-merge-drivers` (one-time, repo-wide -- see
# TCK-20260912-REGISTRY-YAML-MERGE-CONFLICT-TAX) installs a trivial merge driver plus a
# post-merge hook that regenerates automatically. If not installed, a real conflict on
# this path fails the merge loudly (git's own behavior for an unconfigured `merge=`
# attribute) rather than silently taking one side -- resolve by taking either side and
# rerunning `make docs-registry`, same as before.
docs/REGISTRY.yaml merge=registry-regen
